Add optional compiler invocation timing reports

$(TT dub build), $(TT dub run), and $(TT dub test) now accept
$(TT --build-timings). The report is printed to standard error, including with
$(TT --quiet), after the direct build stage and before application or test execution.
If compilation or linking fails, a partial report includes completed and failed
invocations without changing the build's failure status.

-------
dub build --build-mode=singleFile --parallel --build-timings
dub test --build-mode=singleFile --parallel --build-timings
-------

For example, a parallel build of a package named $(TT example) with two source
files printed this timing report:

-------
Build timings (completed)
  Elapsed ms    Start ms      End ms  Status       Phase         Target / sources
     564.390      56.935     621.325  ok           link          example
      51.025       5.605      56.630  ok           compile       example / source/app.d
      49.139       6.097      55.236  ok           compile       example / source/helper.d
  Summed invocation time: 664.554 ms (3 invocations; parallel jobs overlap).
  Build stage elapsed: 624.678 ms (excludes application/test execution).
  Start/end offsets are relative to timing collection start; rows are slowest first.
-------

Rows show elapsed wall time, start/end offsets, exit status, invocation phase,
target package, and explicit source inputs, sorted by decreasing elapsed time.
The status $(TT no status) means that launching or waiting for the process failed
without obtaining an exit status.
Measurements include process launch and waiting for completion, but exclude
worker queueing, command preparation, and output callbacks. Source inputs do not
include every transitively imported module. A combined target's package label
identifies the target owner, not necessarily the owner of every source.

Use $(TT --build-mode=singleFile) for individual source timings. Other modes may
compile multiple sources together or combine compilation and linking. Library
creation may be included in a compile invocation. Cached targets produce no
invocation records; $(TT --force) requests a rebuild. Enabling timings does not
change build cache keys or force recompilation.

Summed invocation time counts overlapping parallel jobs separately. Build-stage
elapsed time includes direct build setup, cache checks, build commands and file
operations, but excludes earlier package configuration, application/test execution,
and report formatting. Start/end offsets begin when the collector is created.

$(TT --rdmd) is unsupported and rejected with this option. Compiler probes and
commands launched by build hooks are not instrumented. Custom compiler
implementations participate when they use DUB's shared process runner.

Use $(TT --build-timings-file=PATH) to write a JSON report. A relative path is
resolved from the directory where you invoke DUB. The parent directory must
exist. New reports are created with owner-only access. Use the file option alone
for JSON, or add $(TT --build-timings) for both
JSON and terminal output:

-------
dub test --build-mode=singleFile --parallel --build-timings --build-timings-file=timings.json
-------

For example, a build that compiled and linked one source file in a single
invocation produced this report (source path shortened):

-------
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "completed": true,
  "buildElapsedNs": 1436907600,
  "summedInvocationNs": 1403419800,
  "invocations": [{
    "package": "example",
    "phase": "compile+link",
    "sources": ["source/app.d"],
    "startOffsetNs": 16764600,
    "endOffsetNs": 1420184400,
    "elapsedNs": 1403419800,
    "exitStatus": 0
  }]
}
-------

Times are integer nanoseconds. Offsets use the same monotonic origin as the
terminal report. Phases are $(TT compile), $(TT link), or $(TT compile+link).
Exit status is an integer, or JSON null if no process status was obtained.

DUB replaces the file after the direct build stage, before running the program
or tests. Cached builds can produce an empty invocation list. When $(TT dub test)
skips a package with target type $(TT none), it writes a completed report with
zero build time and no invocations. A failure in the direct build stage writes
a partial report with $(TT completed) set to false. Failures before that stage,
such as an invalid package recipe, leave any previous report in place. If the
requested file cannot be written, DUB reports the error and exits unsuccessfully.
